<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kyrgyzstan</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Bishkek</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountainous Central Asian republic, once called an 'island of democracy', whose politics have been shaped by three popular revolutions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kirghiz-ssr">Kirghiz SSR</a> declared independence on 31 August 1991 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Askar Akayev</a>, a physicist and reformer who had been elected president in 1990. The new state adopted a constitution in 1993 and its own currency, the som.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Akayev</a>'s early liberal reforms gave way to corruption and family rule. The economy relies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumtor-gold-mine">Kumtor</a> gold mine, agriculture and remittances from migrants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Divisions between the north and the south around Osh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-abad">Jalal-Abad</a> have shaped politics, and in 2010 ethnic violence between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> in Osh killed hundreds. Russia kept an airbase at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kant-kyrgyzstan">Kant</a>, while a US base at Manas operated until 2014.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Protests overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Akayev</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulip-revolution">Tulip Revolution</a> of 2005 and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmanbek-bakiyev">Kurmanbek Bakiyev</a> in 2010, after which a parliamentary system was briefly adopted. After unrest in 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadyr-japarov">Sadyr Japarov</a> became president and restored strong presidential rule by referendum in 2021.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kyrgyzstan.jpg" alt="Kyrgyzstan" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Shakiev N.T., Primov U.B. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kyrgyzstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bishkek</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Askar Akayev, Kurmanbek Bakiyev, Sadyr Japarov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kyrgyz, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Kyrgyzstan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kyrgyz Republic</dd></dl></aside></div>
